    "body": "EICMA 2026 • MTPeak Stole Some of the Spotlight at EICMA 2026 With Insane Power and Retro Looks on Three Wheels\nFirst-Ever Starship Double-Catch Means Rocket Flying Over Populated Texas, Expect Sonic Booms\n31 photos\nPhoto: SpaceX\nIn early October, SpaceX finally completed the first flight to orbit of its Starship. It was the culmination of years of test flights, not all of them successful, but just a stepping stone to what Elon Musk's company describes as the Holy Grail of rocketry: full reusability by recovering, in working condition, both Super Heavy and Starship stages.\nIf the stars align, this historic moment in space exploration could happen as soon as next Friday, November 13, when the 15th flight of the rocket and spacecraft, and the second one to orbit, is scheduled to lift off from Pad 2 at Starbase , Texas.\nSpaceX says the launch window for the mission, which lasts for 75 minutes, opens at 4:45 p.m. CT. If the launch goes as planned, the Starship will spend some 22 hours up there, during which time it will deploy 26 Starlink V3 satellites into orbit. What happens after that, however, could forever rewrite the rules of space launches.\nFlight 15 is when SpaceX will try to catch both stages of the Starship using the Mechazilla launch towers. This would mark the first time an entire orbital system of this size is recovered, which also happens to be SpaceX's ultimate goal.\nDuring the most recent Starship missions, the Super Heavy booster returned to its launch site and was caught by the tower's arms, but the Starship itself went for a splashdown in the ocean. About a week before the flight, SpaceX provided us with the details of Flight 15, and, if you ask me, this is shaping up to be a hell of a flight.\nPhoto: SpaceX You see, a return to the launch site would require the Starship to come back over populated areas, something that has never happened before. It's a pretty tricky approach, as the company needs to make sure nothing goes wrong. And, by the looks of it, all the pieces seem to be in place for the effort to succeed.\nWe're told the flight profile has been imagined with opportunities to safely end the mission early if necessary, and strict criteria that must be met for the final portion of the mission to be given the all-clear.\nFirst up, for most of the mission the spacecraft will use the same suborbital trajectory used during Flight 14. This allows SpaceX to direct the second stage over the Indian Ocean, should something go wrong.\nIf everything is in order, however, Starship will light up a single Raptor sea-level engine to start its orbital insertion burn. Once at its destination, the spacecraft will deploy its payload, and it will then coast for most of the mission, around 21 hours. This is needed so that the Starship aligns itself with the Texas Starbase for return.\nPhoto: SpaceX The bulk of these 21 hours will be spent by ground teams performing all the checks imaginable on the ship's engines, comms systems, flight computers, navigation sensors, and structural integrity, among others.\nShould something not feel right during this stage, teams have several options at their disposal to end the flight early, directing the hardware to designated splashdown areas in the Pacific Ocean, including off Hawaii or Chile.\nIf everything checks out, though, Starship will perform a deorbit burn using a single Raptor engine . This will place it on a trajectory that starts high above the Pacific Ocean, thousands of miles away from the Texas Starbase.\nComing in from the West of the facility, Starship will use its four control flaps to adjust its flight path, which will take it over some populated areas in northern Mexico and southern Texas, but around the major cities of the Rio Grande Valley.\nPhoto: SpaceX Passing to the north of South Padre Island and out over the Gulf of Mexico, it will then make its final turn toward Starbase, which will put some of the people living in and near Cameron County right in the path of some sonic booms.\nWhen it approaches Starbase, the Starship will perform its final bellyflop that will put it en route to Pad 2. Its three sea-level Raptor engines will then ignite, flipping the second stage into a vertical position for the catch, and the flaps will be stowed. A single engine will remain operational as it comes within reach of the tower's mechanical arms, which will then close to secure the spacecraft.\nThe importance of this mission is not lost on SpaceX and all the people involved in the project, as this ability to recover both the booster and the spacecraft is what the company bets on to succeed in helping NASA place human boots on the Moon once more, and, in the mid-term future, send the first crew to Mars.\nAs usual, Starship Flight 15 will be streamed live by SpaceX on its X channel, and we'll be all over it to bring you all the info and the stunning images usually associated with such space missions.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nEditor's note: Gallery shows images from Starship Flight 14.\nspacex starship flight 15\nAbout the author: Daniel Patrascu\nDaniel loves writing (or so he claims), and he uses this skill to offer readers a \"behind the scenes\" look at the automotive industry. He also enjoys talking about space exploration and robots, because in his view the only way forward for humanity is away from this planet, in metal bodies. Full profile\nUP NEXT Ford F-150 Raptor R Meets All-Time Rival Ram 1500 SRT TRX. Which One Is the Faster Truck?",
